BuildKit is a modern build engine used by Docker to build container images faster, more efficiently, and securely. 
It’s designed to improve the traditional Docker build process.

BuildKit is a build engine that takes a configuration file (such as a Dockerfile) and converts it into a built artifact (such as a Docker image). It's faster than Docker's original build engine due to its ability to optimize your build by parallelizing build steps whenever possible and through more advanced layer caching capabilities.

Due to the growing popularity of using computers that are based on non-Intel and AMD CPU architectures, like ARM, it is becoming more and more important that container images can be easily built to support any architecture that an image consumer might be utilizing.

#### How BuildKit Works: 
Parallel Execution: BuildKit can build multiple parts of your Dockerfile at the same time, instead of step-by-step. This significantly speeds up the build.

Better Caching: It remembers the results of previous steps more effectively. So, if you only changed one file, BuildKit skips rebuilding the unchanged parts.

Smaller Image Sizes: BuildKit reduces unnecessary layers in your image, resulting in cleaner, faster builds.

Improved Security: It isolates steps to prevent leaking sensitive data (like environment variables or keys).

On-Demand Dependency Loading: It only pulls the parts of a base image that are needed, reducing download time.


#### With BuildKit:

1. It might copy files and install dependencies in parallel — saving time.
2. It will cache unchanged steps intelligently.

#### To enable BuildKit in Docker, run:
DOCKER_BUILDKIT=1 docker build .